---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import DistaNav from "../../../components/DistaNav.astro";
import { headline, setup, worksToday, notYet, DISTA_REPO } from "../../../data/dista";

const description =
  "dista runs Mojo functions across a cluster: declare a task, call it, and the work spreads over the machines, with results staying where they were computed. Early, open source, and measured against Ray and Rust.";
---

<Base title="dista: distributed tasks for Mojo" description={description}>
  <DistaNav />

  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">dista</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Mojo functions, across a cluster</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        dista is for developers who have written fast Mojo and now need more than one
        machine. A task is an ordinary Mojo function. The program that calls it runs
        unchanged on a laptop, where dista starts its own workers, and on a Kubernetes
        cluster, where every node runs the same small image. There is no Python in the
        path and no second language to learn.
      </p>

      <div class="two-col dista-intro">
        <div class="prose">
          <pre><code>{`from dista import Runtime, Task

def square(x: Int) -> Int:
    return x * x

comptime Square = Task[Int, Int, square]

def main() raises:
    var rt = Runtime[Square](workers=4)
    var r = rt.remote[Square](7)   # an ObjectRef[Int]
    print(rt.get(r))               # 49`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Results that are large stay on the worker that computed them. A task on the same
            host maps them from shared memory, and one elsewhere fetches them from that
            worker directly, without going through the head. A task can take other tasks'
            results as its input, so a pipeline is a graph of calls rather than a sequence of
            round trips to the driver.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="callout">
          <h3 class="callout__title">Who it's for</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Mojo developers</strong> whose code has outgrown one machine, and who want
            to keep it compiled and typed rather than wrap it for a Python scheduler.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Data engineers</strong> scanning Parquet and Iceberg in object storage:
            dista ships with magmalake's native Iceberg reader, and moves Arrow batches
            between tasks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Teams evaluating Ray</strong> for compiled workloads, who want a smaller
            runtime: one binary, a 32 MB image, and a head whose state rebuilds itself after
            a restart.
          </p>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            <a href="/s/dista/docs">Read the docs</a> ·
            <a href={DISTA_REPO} rel="noopener">source on GitHub</a>
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Performance so far</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">
        {headline.dista} s a pass, against {headline.rust} s for Rust and {headline.ray} s for Ray
      </h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The same eight queries over 1.4 billion taxi trips in an Iceberg table in S3, on
        three worker nodes, with the answers checked against each other.
        A day earlier dista took {headline.before} s on the same run; rewriting how it reads
        from object storage made it the fastest of the three.
        The <a href="/s/dista/performance">performance page</a> has every query, the
        before-and-after timeline, the earlier runs where dista lost, and how to reproduce
        each number.
      </p>
      <div class="dista-bars" role="img" aria-label={`dista ${headline.dista} s, Rust ${headline.rust} s, Ray ${headline.ray} s`}>
        {
          [
            { name: "dista", s: headline.dista },
            { name: "Rust", s: headline.rust },
            { name: "Ray", s: headline.ray },
          ].map((b) => (
            <div class="dista-bar">
              <span class="dista-bar__name">{b.name}</span>
              <span class="dista-bar__track">
                <span
                  class={`dista-bar__fill${b.name === "dista" ? " dista-bar__fill--lead" : ""}`}
                  style={`width: ${(b.s / headline.ray) * 100}%`}
                />
              </span>
              <span class="dista-bar__value num">{b.s} s</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="panel__fine dista-fine">
        Median pass of the eight queries, 3 × {setup.machine.split(",")[0]}, {setup.date}. Lower is better.
        One run on one day: the comparison frameworks varied about 10% between runs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Status</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Early, and measured as it goes</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        dista is in its third phase: tasks, results and a head that restarts are done;
        stateful actors arrived this week and are being made to survive restarts. What it
        doesn't do yet is listed here rather than left to be found out.
      </p>
      <div class="status-grid">
        <div class="status-col status-col--yes">
          <h3 class="status-col__head">Works today</h3>
          <ul>{worksToday.map((w) => <li>{w}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div class="status-col status-col--no">
          <h3 class="status-col__head">Not yet</h3>
          <ul>{notYet.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="panel__fine dista-fine">
        A longer write-up, with the design and the measurements behind it, follows when the
        actor work is complete.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .dista-intro { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .dista-intro pre { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  .dista-bars {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  .dista-bar {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 4rem 1fr 4.5rem;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }
  .dista-bar__name { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8125rem; }
  .dista-bar__track {
    height: 0.85rem;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .dista-bar__fill {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    background: var(--text-faint);
  }
  .dista-bar__fill--lead { background: var(--ember); }
  .dista-bar__value { text-align: right; }
  .dista-fine { margin-top: 1rem; max-width: var(--measure); }
</style>
